1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: How can a quarter turn give you more than half?

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: The rod tilts. That makes the piston hurry through the top half of the stroke, and dawdle through the bottom.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: Okay. Let's run one whole cycle, slowly, from the top.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: Stroke one is intake. The intake valve opens, and the falling piston draws in air and fuel.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: It's pulled in by the partial vacuum it leaves behind.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: Stroke 2 is compression. Both valves shut, and the rising piston squeezes the mixture into about a tenth of the space.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: And by now the crankshaft has turned once, all the way round.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Near the top, the spark plug fires, and the mixture starts to burn.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: Stroke three is power. The hot, high-pressure gas drives the piston down.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: And that's the only stroke that pushes on the crank.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: The other three just get the gas in, ready, and out again.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: Stroke 4 is exhaust. The exhaust valve opens, and the rising piston pushes the burnt gas out.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: Then it all starts again. That's four strokes, two turns of the crank, and one push.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: That's why the camshaft turns at half the crankshaft's speed, so that each valve opens once per cycle.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: So it's two turns of the crank for one turn of the cam.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: But what carries the piston through the three strokes that don't push?

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: It's a heavy flywheel. It soaks up energy from each power stroke, and gives some back to squeeze the next charge.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: So who came up with all this? The idea is older than the engine that made it famous.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: In 1860, Étienne Lenoir in Paris built a gas engine that burned its fuel without compressing it first.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: And it turned only about four percent of the fuel's energy into work.
